  "notes": "Philadelphia Def Soul tenor on the Carvin & Ivan / Dre & Vidal board, and the entry explains the split personality of the numbers: \"his two producers came up as hip-hop artists first, which is why the rhythm layer stays gridded while the harmony does not\". So the drums are the most quantised in this batch — a hard 2&4 with zero snare displacement, a swung 16th hat line and a live-feel percussion overlay — while the chords carry the stated \"live-instrument, chord-rich Philly-soul bed\" with \"4-chord cycles with 7ths/9ths and passing chords, not vamps\". The band is the entry's own 71–123, one of the widest in the lane \"because the label pushed uptempo singles\", and the keys are the named F♯ minor, D♭ minor, B♭ minor and B minor against C and A♭ major. The harmony families are the two the research writes out. Melody is the identifying claim: \"scat/beatbox-derived rhythmic phrasing — he came up scatting in jazz clubs — so the top line is syncopated and busy against a simple chord bed, with runs placed on off-16ths\", which is the high density, the short phrase length and the question-answer structure. The bass is the stated walking electric at the stated E1–B2, so its register tops out at 47 and the chord voicing starts at 48. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity, the humanize values, and the melody/countermelody numbers — the research gives adjectives (\"hard\", \"swung\", \"busy\"), not figures. ⚠ The 2007–2008 synth-sub doubling the entry mentions is not encoded as an 808 block: it doubles a played bass rather than replacing it, and authoring `drums.bass808` would silence the bassline generator.",
